# ACT ONE

## Scene One

A cramped community theatre green room, late at night. A single bare
bulb hangs from the ceiling. Two mismatched armchairs face each other.

*MARGARET*, sixties, sharp-eyed, sits mending a costume. *DAVID*, thirty,
paces.

DAVID
I can't do the monologue. Not tonight. Not after what happened.

MARGARET
(not looking up)
You said that before the read-through, too.

DAVID
This is different.

MARGARET
It's always different.

David stops pacing. He looks at the door, then back at her.

DAVID
What if I forget it? In front of everyone?

MARGARET
Then you'll remember it's a play, and the audience already knows how
it ends.

A long silence. Somewhere in the building, a door slams.

DAVID
(quietly)
I don't know how it ends. That's the problem.

> LIGHTS SHIFT TO:

## Scene Two

The stage itself, empty except for a single chair under a work light.

MARGARET enters alone and sits.

MARGARET
Some nights the theatre is the only honest room left.

She looks out at the empty seats.

MARGARET
(to the house)
You're not here yet. But you will be.

===

# ACT TWO

## Scene One

>THE FOLLOWING WEEK<

The green room again, tidier now. DAVID enters, already in costume.

DAVID
I remembered all of it.

MARGARET
I never doubted you.

DAVID
Liar.

They laugh. Blackout.
